31 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180322 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180322 #edb/ref 1061 138022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180322 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba8eac6e-a6b0-5050-a5e7-2508d56d4806" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180322 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180323 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180323 #edb/ref 1061 138023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180323 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8393e8f1-d3a4-51f5-bea6-a2c3546128e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180323 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180324 #edb/ref 1061 138026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180324 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a721917-d440-59e3-a3b3-441903fcc6b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180324 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180783 #edb/ref 1064 "To determine the slope of a line that is given in standard form, we need to convert the equation to slope-intercept form.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's calculate the slope of the following line:\n\n$$\n2x + y = 8\n$$\n\nTo write this equation in slope-intercept form, we solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + y &= 8 \\\\\n2x + y - 2x &= 8 - 2x \\\\\ny &= 8 - 2x \\\\\ny &= -2x + 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the slope-intercept equation, we can tell that the slope of the line is $m=-2$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180783 #edb/ref 1065 8697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180783 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3550ae2f-df69-52cc-b33a-14e8f2bb2d8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180783 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180784 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to find the $x$- and $y$-intercepts of the following line:\n\n$$\n2x + y = 8\n$$\n\nLet's find each of them in turn.\n\n- To find the $x$-intercept, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation and solve for $x{:}$\n$2x + (0)|= 8; 2x|= 8; \\frac{2x}{2}|= \\frac{8}{2}; x|= 4$\nTherefore, the line intersects the $x$-axis at the point $(4,0)$.\n\n- To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation and solve for $y{:}$\n$2(0) + y|= 8; y|= 8$\nTherefore, the line intersects the $y$-axis at the point $(0,8)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180784 #edb/ref 1065 8699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180784 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c500072d-6462-54c2-8c16-3c8f68bdbbfe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180784 #edb/ref 1067 "Determining Intercepts of Lines Given in Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
